<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-valine degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="VALDEG-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: VAL| is one of the three main branched chain amino acids (BCAAs), along with |FRAME: LEU| and |FRAME: ILE|. The catabolic pathways of the BCAAs can be divided into two sequential series of reactions, referred to as the common pathway and the distal pathway. The common pathway includes the enzymes |FRAME: CPLX-6941|, |FRAME: CPLX8J2-279|, and |FRAME: CPLX-6942|. These three enzymes catalyze the conversion of all three BCAAs to their respective acyl-CoA derivatives (|FRAME:METHACRYLYL-COA|, |FRAME:3-METHYL-CROTONYL-COA|, and |FRAME: CPD-1083| for |FRAME:VAL|, |FRAME:LEU|, and |FRAME:ILE|, respectively), though the intermediates formed by these enzymes are different for the different amino acids. The distal parts of the pathway are completely different for the three BCAAs, and comprise enzymes specific for each amino acid |CITS: [773366]|. The catabolism of |FRAME: VAL| involves its conversion via |FRAME:2-KETO-ISOVALERATE| to |FRAME:ISOBUTYRYL-COA|, which is desaturated to form the &amp;alpha;,&amp;beta;-unsaturated thioester |FRAME:METHACRYLYL-COA|. Hydration to |FRAME:3-HYDROXY-ISOBUTYRYL-COA| and thioester hydrolysis forms the diffusible and transportable compound |FRAME:3-HYDROXY-ISOBUTYRATE|. Finally, this &amp;beta;-hydroxy acid is oxidized to |FRAME:CH3-MALONATE-S-ALD| and thioesterified to form |FRAME:PROPIONYL-COA|. The pathway is widely distributed and has been demonstrated in bacteria |CITS: [4957438][5030618]|, fungi, cilliates |CITS: [13457352]|, higher plants |CITS: [Gerbling88][15280033]| and animals |CITS: [13233241][13398382][8824301]|. A few exceptions are |FRAME: TAX-83333|, which does not possess a |FRAME: CPLX8J2-279|, and |FRAME: TAX-4932|, which cannot use |FRAME: VAL| as a carbon source, and degrades it into |FRAME: ISOBUTANOL| (see |FRAME:PWY-5057|). In eukaryotic organisms the pathway spans both the cytoplasmic and mitochondrial compartments, as the first reaction takes place in the cytoplasm, while its product, |FRAME: 2-KETO-ISOVALERATE|, is transported into the mitochondria, where the rest of the pathway occurs.</Description>
